          "title": "How coral reefs repair themselves",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "label": "A",
              "text": "Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support around a quarter of all marine species."
            },
            {
              "label": "B",
              "text": "When water temperatures rise, corals expel the algae that live in their tissues and turn white, a process known as bleaching."
            },
            {
              "label": "C",
              "text": "Bleached corals are not dead. If temperatures return to normal within a few weeks, the algae can return."
            },
            {
              "label": "D",
              "text": "Some reefs recover faster than others. Researchers found that reefs with many fish recover more quickly."
            },
            {
              "label": "E",
              "text": "Fish that graze on seaweed keep the surface clear, giving young corals space to settle."
