          "title": "Railways and standard time",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "label": "A",
              "text": "Before the railways, each town in Britain kept its own local time, set by the position of the sun."
            },
            {
              "label": "B",
              "text": "Fast trains made these differences a practical problem, because timetables could not be printed clearly."
            },
            {
              "label": "C",
              "text": "In 1840 one railway company adopted London time across its entire network."
            },
            {
              "label": "D",
              "text": "Other companies soon followed, and public clocks began to show two minute hands."
            },
            {
              "label": "E",
              "text": "Some towns resisted, arguing that local time was part of their identity."
