        {
          "title": "How babies learn grammar",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "label": "A",
              "text": "Children learn grammar long before anyone teaches them rules."
            },
            {
              "label": "B",
              "text": "Researchers once believed that imitation explained most of this learning."
            },
            {
              "label": "C",
              "text": "However, young children produce forms they have never heard, such as 'goed' instead of 'went'."
            },
            {
              "label": "D",
              "text": "These errors suggest that children build general patterns and then apply them too widely."
            },
            {
              "label": "E",
              "text": "Studies with invented words support this view."
            },
            {
              "label": "F",
              "text": "Most researchers now agree that both experience and pattern-finding play a role."
